<% static = static_podcast_state(@source) %>
<div class="flex max-w-4xl flex-col gap-6 text-black dark:text-white">
  <p class="max-w-prose text-sm text-bodydark">
    Any source can be subscribed to as a podcast. There are two ways to serve it — pick whichever
    matches how your podcast app reaches this machine.
  </p>

  <%!--
    The two delivery modes, most-available first: the dynamic feed always works
    (Tubeless serves it itself, auth-bypassed for podcast apps), while the static
    one depends on the user having stood up their own web server.
  --%>
  <section class="rounded-xs border border-stroke dark:border-strokedark">
    <h3 class="border-b border-stroke px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-bodydark dark:border-strokedark">
      Subscribe
    </h3>
    <div class="divide-y divide-stroke dark:divide-strokedark">
      <.feed_option icon="hero-signal" title="Served by Tubeless" badge="Ready" url={rss_feed_url(@conn, @source)}>
        <:description>
          Tubeless generates and serves this feed itself, so it works with no extra setup — as long
          as your podcast app can reach this system. The URL bypasses the app's login on purpose so
          podcast apps can read it. This option is suitable for LAN only. DO NOT expose Tubeless to the internet.
        </:description>
      </.feed_option>

      <.feed_option
        icon="hero-server-stack"
        title="Served by your own web server"
        badge={if static.url, do: "Ready"}
        url={static.url}
      >
        <:description>
          Tubeless writes a static <code class="font-mono text-xs">feed.xml</code>
          next to the downloaded episodes. A separate
          static web server (nginx, Caddy, …) is required to serve your library files.
          This solution decouples Tubeless from your web server to provide higher security.
        </:description>
        <:unavailable :if={!static.publishing}>
          This source isn't published as a static podcast, so no files are written for a web server
          to serve. Turn on <strong>Publish as Podcast</strong>
          in its media profile
          <.subtle_link href={~p"/media_profiles/#{@source.media_profile_id}"}>({@source.media_profile.name})</.subtle_link>
          to enable it. The feed above works either way.
        </:unavailable>
        <:unavailable :if={static.missing_url_base}>
          The feed URL cannot be built until the
          <.subtle_link href={~p"/settings"}>Podcast URL Base setting</.subtle_link>
          is set — Tubeless doesn't know the address your web server address.
        </:unavailable>
        <:unavailable :if={static.pending}>
          The feed is still being generated in the background. Its URL appears here once
          <code class="font-mono text-xs">feed.xml</code>
          has been written — check the <.subtle_link href={~p"/diagnostics"}>job diagnostics</.subtle_link>
          if it hasn't shown up after the first downloads finish.
        </:unavailable>
      </.feed_option>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section :if={static.url || static.pending}>
    <h3 class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-bodydark">Files on disk</h3>
    <p class="mb-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-bodydark">
      Tubeless writes this source's feed, cover art, and episodes here. This is the directory your
      static web server should be serving.
    </p>
    <code class="break-all font-mono text-sm">{static.local_path}</code>
  </section>

  <p class="text-sm text-bodydark">
    <.icon name="hero-book-open" class="mr-1 h-4 w-4" />
    <%!-- Kept on one line: newlines inside the anchor render as underlined whitespace. --%>
    <.subtle_link
      href="https://github.com/CommunityMaintained/tubeless/wiki/Podcast-RSS-Feeds"
      target="_blank"
    >Podcast RSS Feeds wiki page</.subtle_link>
    — how to serve the library and what each mode is good for.
  </p>
</div>
